WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.207
Migrating legacy string code should be
deliberate.

2
00:00:03.287 --> 00:00:06.609
Start by inventorying call sites and
grouping them by behavior:

3
00:00:06.689 --> 00:00:10.258
copying, concatenation, formatting,
parsing, path construction,

4
00:00:10.338 --> 00:00:13.045
protocol fields, logging, and dynamic
allocation.

5
00:00:13.125 --> 00:00:16.816
This gives the team a map of where risk
and complexity are concentrated.

6
00:00:16.896 --> 00:00:20.280
Prioritize paths where string behavior
affects trust, parsing,

7
00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.806
storage, configuration, external input, or
resource selection.

8
00:00:23.886 --> 00:00:27.823
A low-risk display-only buffer may not
need the same urgency as a parser that

9
00:00:27.903 --> 00:00:29.441
controls application state.

10
00:00:29.521 --> 00:00:33.643
Migration should focus first on code where
hidden assumptions have the highest

11
00:00:33.723 --> 00:00:34.154
impact.

12
00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:35.834
Add tests before replacement.

13
00:00:35.914 --> 00:00:40.159
Existing behavior may be flawed, but it is
still behavior the system may depend on.

14
00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:44.484
Boundary tests, truncation tests,
empty-string tests, malformed-input tests,

15
00:00:44.564 --> 00:00:48.994
and ownership tests help reveal whether
the new API changes semantics intentionally

16
00:00:49.074 --> 00:00:49.997
or accidentally.

17
00:00:50.077 --> 00:00:52.046
Avoid blind mechanical replacement.

18
00:00:52.126 --> 00:00:55.017
A new function may have different
truncation behavior,

19
00:00:55.097 --> 00:00:59.158
termination guarantees, return values,
error codes, allocation ownership,

20
00:00:59.238 --> 00:01:00.099
or portability.

21
00:01:00.179 --> 00:01:04.363
Replacing names without reviewing
semantics can make the code look modern while

22
00:01:04.443 --> 00:01:06.596
changing the program in unintended ways.

23
00:01:06.676 --> 00:01:09.383
Migrate incrementally with approved
conventions.

24
00:01:09.463 --> 00:01:12.970
Introduce project wrappers where they
clarify repeated behavior.

25
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:14.957
Document how truncation is handled.

26
00:01:15.037 --> 00:01:16.452
Update review checklists.

27
00:01:16.532 --> 00:01:19.117
Keep changes small enough to test and
understand.

28
00:01:19.197 --> 00:01:20.981
The point is not a cosmetic rename;

29
00:01:21.061 --> 00:01:24.260
the point is to make string behavior
explicit and repeatable.
